WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.860
Bad medical advice? Rich, neglectful&nbsp;
parents? "CoComelon" has a lot more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.260
going on under the surface than children realize!

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.300
Although many kids excitedly shriek&nbsp;
"CoComelon" at the screen every time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.160
they see those two front teeth and that single&nbsp;
blonde swirl, "CoComelon" is actually the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.400
of the YouTube Channel and streaming&nbsp;
show. The kids are on the right track,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.960
as the blonde baby is the main character and&nbsp;
his face has become synonymous with the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.700
but hordes of toddlers around the world have been&nbsp;
surprised to learn that his name is actually JJ.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.840
The confusion is completely warranted&nbsp;
considering the titles of many popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.640
kids' shows are simply the name of the main&nbsp;
character. "Bluey," "Peppa Pig," "Winnie the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.480
Pooh," "Daniel Tiger's Neighborhood,"&nbsp;
and "Arthur" are just a few examples.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.060
JJ, his Mommy, Daddy, his sister YoYo, his&nbsp;
brother TomTom, and a slew of other friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.200
family, and animals collectively make&nbsp;
up the world of "CoComelon." Granted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.280
many parents are confused by this as well,&nbsp;
but the fact that multiple videos that only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.320
featured animals were also titled "CoComelon"&nbsp;
became the dead giveaway. Questions about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.400
name were raised and ultimately answered with&nbsp;
songs like "The Potty Training Song," where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.560
the family specifically says, "Great job, JJ!"&nbsp;
Then, of course, there's the "JJ song" itself.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:16.020
This one seems to affect parents in a big way.&nbsp;
Throughout the entire "CoComelon" catalog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:20.940
JJ displays the broadest spectrum of cognitive&nbsp;
abilities. While enjoying the show, it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:25.260
be near impossible for toddlers to pick up on&nbsp;
the timing of developmental stages. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:28.860
parents are increasingly more aware of the&nbsp;
many leaps their children should be making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:33.240
around certain ages. The Huffington Post&nbsp;
gathered numerous parental tweets about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.500
"Cocomelon," and many call out JJ's&nbsp;
extraordinary abilities in a big way.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:43.080
JJ's actual age has been estimated at anywhere&nbsp;
from 1 to 4 years old. But in some cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.880
his intelligence would place him lower than a 1&nbsp;
year old, and in others, a lot higher than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:53.220
average 4 year old. In the episode "Baby Bump,"&nbsp;
JJ and his best friend Cody act as detectives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:59.100
and uncover clues like a rattle, a pacifier, and a&nbsp;
baby bottle to come to the conclusion that Cody's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.840
mom must be pregnant. In the episode "Humpty&nbsp;
Dumpty," JJ giggles and babbles like a baby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.875
but also engineers a parachute for&nbsp;
an egg in an egg drop experiment.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:12.000
Mommy and Daddy's income is hopefully&nbsp;
something that a show's younger viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.020
don't have to think about. Even so, after&nbsp;
scoping the family's house and the laundry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:20.460
list of fun activities they're able to do with&nbsp;
the kids, more than a few older viewers have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:24.540
started wondering how JJ's parents are&nbsp;
able to afford their lavish lifestyle.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.680
Parents in the audience have discussed the&nbsp;
house itself, since the color, furniture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:34.140
and overall layout of each room changes song to&nbsp;
song. In the parents version of the song "Johny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:39.120
Johny Yes Papa," the parents are seen in a quaint&nbsp;
kitchen with pictures of vegetables decorating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.280
the walls. But in "Pat A Cake 2," the family&nbsp;
walks into a bright yellow beehive kitchen. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:49.380
initial version of "Johny Johny Yes Papa" shows a&nbsp;
third version of their kitchen, which raises the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.760
question, "Is this family that well-off, or are&nbsp;
they good friends with Chip and Joanna Gaines?"

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.580
"Took it out 100%. Zero breaks."

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:57.360
"This is nice."

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.580
With childcare at the forefront of any parent's&nbsp;
mind, even the cost of JJ's pristine preschool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.720
the Melon Patch Academy, has been&nbsp;
called into question. Not that JJ's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.200
parents need to explain themselves,&nbsp;
but their jobs are never mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.117
and they always seem to have ample&nbsp;
time off to play with the kids.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.700
Judging other parents' child rearing? Now&nbsp;
that's something every parent can get behind.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.580
"Oh! You are not telling&nbsp;
me how to raise my child."

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:23.100
"You do not tell this woman&nbsp;
how to raise her child."

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.080
"You do not tell her how to raise me."

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.840
Kids definitely wouldn't notice&nbsp;
this, and even if they did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:32.040
they'd most likely be a little jealous&nbsp;
of JJ and his siblings' freedom. With the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.520
children being left unattended, bouncing&nbsp;
around town without parental supervision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:40.140
it's easy for any parent to wonder about the&nbsp;
lackadaisical rules in this family's household.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:45.120
The "Car Wash Song" video, for example, suggests&nbsp;
that a preschool trio can wash a car unattended.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.680
The whole thing will strike any parent viewing as&nbsp;
even more irresponsible when they notice that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:53.820
kids are doing this only steps away from a&nbsp;
busy road with double solid yellow lines.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:57.780
On a similar note, in the "Breakfast&nbsp;
Song," the children concoct giant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:03.600
unhealthy breakfasts consisting entirely of&nbsp;
pancakes, syrup, waffles, whipped cream, bacon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.195
ham, and white bread with jam. It's all fried up&nbsp;
on a hot stove with zero parental supervision.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:14.520
Many of the things only parents notice about&nbsp;
"CoComelon" are nitpicks within the world of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.720
the show, but they aren't all bad. For example,&nbsp;
there's the "CoComelon" rendition of the classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.060
song "Pat-A-Cake," which has educated many parents&nbsp;
who grew up singing it as "Patty Cake." Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:27.960
other kids and parents may have only ever&nbsp;
known the song as "Pat-A-Cake." But for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:31.440
those who've been singing "Patty Cake"&nbsp;
all along, you can take comfort in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.340
fact that this pronunciation is very&nbsp;
common, and widely accepted as well.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:40.980
"Pat-A-Cake" versus "Patty Cake" has been debated&nbsp;
for decades. But experts like Sunn m'Cheaux — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:45.540
Gullah language teacher from Harvard University&nbsp;
— claim that "Pat-a" was pronounced "Patty" in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.500
different languages and dialects. As long&nbsp;
as the cake makers in question mold it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.880
pat it, and mark it with a B, it seems&nbsp;
both pronunciations have some merit.

00:04:54.454 --> 00:04:58.140
"CoComelon" has served up fresh&nbsp;
renditions of a lot of nursery rhymes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:01.920
some of which they've opted to put their own&nbsp;
unique spin on. When children like a song,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:07.080
they don't question its origins. However, parents&nbsp;
tuning into "CoComelon" will be likely to notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:12.120
distinct similarities with songs that they, their&nbsp;
parents, and even their grandparents grew up with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:15.840
"Winter Show and Tell at School" is sung to&nbsp;
the tune of "On the First Day of Christmas."

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.240
"Bath Song" is the most popular video on&nbsp;
the "CoComelon" channel with nearly 5.8&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:25.140
billion views. While it has original&nbsp;
lyrics, the verse is sung to the tune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:28.980
of "Itsy Bitsy Spider" and the chorus&nbsp;
has the same melody as "Baby Shark."

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:32.640
Children's songs performed to the tune&nbsp;
of classic nursery rhymes are actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.320
quite common. Some of our most well-known&nbsp;
nursery rhymes, like "ABC," "Twinkle Twinkle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:42.208
Little Star," and "Baa Baa Black Sheep" are&nbsp;
actually all sung in the same tune and tempo.

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:45.360
These songs and some variations composed by&nbsp;
Mozart all share the common ancestor of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:50.880
hit 1740 French folk song. Much like a movie&nbsp;
or TV character watching a movie or TV show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:55.620
from several decades ago, current children's&nbsp;
songs that use classic nursery rhymes are more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:59.340
often than not doing so to avoid copyright&nbsp;
infringement. Not to mention the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:03.601
it helps a new song's chances of success.&nbsp;
The classics are classics for a reason.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.260
While your child blankly stares at the&nbsp;
screen with their jaw on the floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:11.220
you can rest assured they're not counting the&nbsp;
seconds of each shot. For parents who are trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:14.760
to find the proper balance between entertaining&nbsp;
their children and melting their brains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:19.440
the pace of a show has become very important.&nbsp;
There is no shortage of new shows vying for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.180
the attention of young viewers, but when parents&nbsp;
and experts are asked if "CoComelon" specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:28.860
is safe or hyper-stimulating for children, the&nbsp;
answers seem to be split right down the middle.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:34.500
For comparison, a TikTok user clocked "My Little&nbsp;
Pony" shots as lasting 4 to 6 seconds each. Each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:39.060
"CoComelon" shot, on the other hand, only&nbsp;
lasts 1 to 3 seconds in length. This video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:42.600
as well as a follow-up video showing&nbsp;
that nearly every "CoComelon" shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:48.420
either zooms in or out or pans side to side,&nbsp;
inspired over 300 parents to weigh in on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:52.320
"Parenting" Subreddit. Many commented that&nbsp;
their children watched the show obsessively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:57.240
and would become unusually upset after&nbsp;
it was turned off. Dr. Rebecca G. Cowan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:02.580
a professor of social and behavioral sciences&nbsp;
at Walden University, told Parenting.com,

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:06.540
"Without empirical research on&nbsp;
the show 'CoComelon,' there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:12.060
no data to substantiate claims that this show is&nbsp;
overstimulating due to the pace of the scenes."

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:15.840
It seems that until more structured&nbsp;
research is performed, it will be up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.399
to parents whether or not it's healthy&nbsp;
for their children to view the show. .

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:24.060
For the average young viewer, it's unlikely that&nbsp;
children will pick up on the subtle nuances of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:29.280
a couple's relationship in a TV show or YouTube&nbsp;
video. After watching the video for the parents'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:33.720
version of "Johny Johny Yes Papa," it isn't&nbsp;
hard for any adult viewer to pick up on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:38.640
sneaking around, the distrust, the judgment,&nbsp;
and the outright lying present in the clip.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:43.080
The video opens on the father sneaking into the&nbsp;
kitchen in the middle of the night for a slice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:47.640
of pizza. The mother then flicks on the light and&nbsp;
judges him as if he's straying from their strict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:52.560
diet. The father then catches and judges her for&nbsp;
enjoying some ice cream. It gets a tad off-putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:57.000
when they both lie about it twice, and ultimately&nbsp;
need to open their mouths to expose the lie.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.960
"CoComelon's" first version of "Johny&nbsp;
Johny Yes Papa" is more true to form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:06.360
with JJ playing Johny, getting caught eating&nbsp;
sugar, and lying about it. The father finds it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:10.200
cute that JJ says he wasn't eating sugar&nbsp;
when he clearly has some in his mouth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:14.392
but the cuteness just isn't there when the&nbsp;
parents treat each other like children.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:18.660
Young viewers clearly don't have too&nbsp;
critical of an outlook toward media yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:22.560
because they seem to be all in on the&nbsp;
many characters of "CoComelon." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:26.340
many parents have to admit being completely&nbsp;
creeped out by the slightly haunting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:29.631
almost Chucky-like facial&nbsp;
expressions of these characters.

00:08:29.631 --> 00:08:33.236
"I don't believe it. I just don't believe it."

00:08:33.236 --> 00:08:34.260
For a little over a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:38.160
Collider contributor Matt Rooney lived&nbsp;
with his brother, his brother's partner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:43.200
and their first child. After being exposed to&nbsp;
countless hours of "CoComelon," he reported,

00:09:02.334 --> 00:09:06.780
Thanks to films like "The Exorcist,” "The&nbsp;
Shining," "Children of the Corn," and "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:10.980
Ring," there is no shortage of horror movies&nbsp;
involving creepy children. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:14.220
the concept is basically its own&nbsp;
horror sub-genre. For this reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:18.180
many adults find something terrifying&nbsp;
about "CoComelon"'s uncanny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:22.080
lifeless smiles. While it's innocent enough&nbsp;
to keep children from running for the hills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:26.913
something about the show's imagery wouldn't feel&nbsp;
out of place in a contemporary horror movie.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:31.740
When a toddler gets a boo boo, they usually want&nbsp;
no part in the washing and dressing of the wound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:36.360
but the parents know an infection could set in&nbsp;
quickly. In looking over the few instances of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:40.560
first-aid in "CoComelon," it doesn't take&nbsp;
a medical professional to see the issues.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:45.600
Doctor Mike Varshavski, a Board certified Family&nbsp;
Medicine practitioner and the number one health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:50.640
and medicine influencer on YouTube, reviewed "The&nbsp;
Boo Boo Song." He noticed that when Cody falls and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:55.560
scrapes his knee, his mother simply puts a bandage&nbsp;
over it without washing or disinfecting the wound.

00:09:55.560 --> 00:10:00.180
Other red flags from "CoComelon"'s depiction&nbsp;
of medical care include putting a bandage on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:04.140
a stubbed toe and listening to a patient's&nbsp;
lungs from the front instead of the back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:08.580
The most egregious mistake of all, though,&nbsp;
is the use of an otoscope — the very pointy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:13.200
tool used to examine the patient's ear — on&nbsp;
the patient's eye. Doctor Mike warns viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:17.034
to use the very flat ophthalmoscope&nbsp;
for eye inspections, and stating,

00:10:17.034 --> 00:10:20.040
"If you do that on a medical&nbsp;
standardized exam, you would fail!"

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:24.240
Additionally, when JJ gets sick in "Sick&nbsp;
Song," his parents care for him. When his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.860
mom sneezes at the end of the video, JJ and his&nbsp;
father laugh. Parents everywhere will tell you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:32.280
how often they've been sick because of their&nbsp;
kids, and would not laugh along with this.

